00:08:37.220 --> 00:08:38.860
It was a process, but we did it.

00:08:38.860 --> 00:08:40.030
- Here McKamey tells her

00:08:40.030 --> 00:08:41.510
that she won't be injured or killed,

00:08:41.510 --> 00:08:43.330
but may end up with bumps and bruises

00:08:43.330 --> 00:08:44.800
and even a broken bone.

00:08:44.800 --> 00:08:47.070
- [McKamey] You know that
no ones gonna kill you.

00:08:47.070 --> 00:08:47.903
- Yeah.

00:08:47.903 --> 00:08:49.670
- [McKamey] You know that
you're not gonna get injured.

00:08:49.670 --> 00:08:52.450
But you also know that you're very likely,

00:08:52.450 --> 00:08:54.560
in fact, I can guarantee you

00:08:54.560 --> 00:08:55.970
that you're going to get bumps

00:08:55.970 --> 00:08:58.470
and bruises and sprains,

00:08:58.470 --> 00:09:00.290
maybe even a broken bone.

00:09:00.290 --> 00:09:02.340
- Russ should probably
know that bumps and bruises

00:09:02.340 --> 00:09:04.660
and broken bones are in fact injuries,

00:09:04.660 --> 00:09:06.340
but we'll leave that aside for the moment.

00:09:06.340 --> 00:09:10.750
- [McKamey] There's some
bruises (indistinct)

00:09:10.750 --> 00:09:12.083
That's what you get.

00:09:12.083 --> 00:09:12.916
(laughs)

00:09:12.916 --> 00:09:13.749
[McKamey] Man, it looks good.

00:09:13.749 --> 00:09:15.430
- And he had Beth agrees on camera

00:09:15.430 --> 00:09:17.070
before she even starts the event.

00:09:17.070 --> 00:09:18.120
- [McKamey] We never had a broken bone,

00:09:18.120 --> 00:09:19.210
but maybe you're the first, right?

00:09:19.210 --> 00:09:20.998
- Maybe I'll be the record breaker.

00:09:20.998 --> 00:09:24.240
- [McKamey] (indistinct)
the actual real breaker.

00:09:24.240 --> 00:09:26.330
- Did she sign the waiver then to?

00:09:26.330 --> 00:09:27.300
McKamey implies that she did.

00:09:27.300 --> 00:09:28.980
We know he has a video

00:09:28.980 --> 00:09:31.310
of him reading the various
parts of this waiver.

00:09:31.310 --> 00:09:32.200
And it's also possible

00:09:32.200 --> 00:09:35.030
that these are just actors (laughs).

00:09:35.030 --> 00:09:37.670
And it's just really
really good marketing.

00:09:37.670 --> 00:09:39.080
But later in the same video,

00:09:39.080 --> 00:09:40.330
McKamey interviews Beth

00:09:40.330 --> 00:09:42.220
after the events depicted in the video

00:09:42.220 --> 00:09:44.040
asking did we ever force you

00:09:44.040 --> 00:09:46.200
to which she replies absolutely not.

00:09:46.200 --> 00:09:48.140
- [McKamey] And you know
that it's physical forehand.

00:09:48.140 --> 00:09:48.973
You know, all these things, right?

00:09:48.973 --> 00:09:50.599
So do we keep any secrets from you -

00:09:50.599 --> 00:09:51.802
- Absolutely not.

00:09:51.802 --> 00:09:52.635
- Without telling you?

00:09:52.635 --> 00:09:53.510
- But is this truly a situation

00:09:53.510 --> 00:09:55.480
where Beth feels that she has no choice

00:09:55.480 --> 00:09:56.870
but to sign the contract?

00:09:56.870 --> 00:09:58.460
Is she being compelled by force?

00:09:58.460 --> 00:09:59.780
And then the threat of force.

00:09:59.780 --> 00:10:01.860
We don't really know for sure,

00:10:01.860 --> 00:10:03.410
because the video cuts to a clip

00:10:03.410 --> 00:10:04.920
of Beth before she enters the

00:10:04.920 --> 00:10:06.990
event where she's pretty obviously capable

00:10:06.990 --> 00:10:07.910
of signing the waiver.

00:10:07.910 --> 00:10:10.040
And here's Beth later saying that yes

00:10:10.040 --> 00:10:11.890
she went through page by page.

00:10:11.890 --> 00:10:13.600
- It was a process but we did it.

00:10:13.600 --> 00:10:15.110
- According to Beth,
she signed the paperwork

00:10:15.110 --> 00:10:17.410
before the experience and
acquiesced to it ahead of time.

00:10:17.410 --> 00:10:18.250
And obviously if people

00:10:18.250 --> 00:10:19.780
didn't sign the waiver ahead of time

00:10:19.780 --> 00:10:21.710
and aren't informed about
what's about to happen.

00:10:21.710 --> 00:10:24.710
Then yes, McKamey might
have all sorts of liability.

00:10:24.710 --> 00:10:26.230
So if McKamey beat them

00:10:26.230 --> 00:10:27.580
or held them at gunpoint

00:10:27.580 --> 00:10:29.320
or use some sort of economic coercion

00:10:29.320 --> 00:10:31.200
against them when they sign the waiver,

00:10:31.200 --> 00:10:32.300
then I'm confident the waiver

00:10:32.300 --> 00:10:33.780
would probably be invalid. (laughs)

00:10:33.780 --> 00:10:35.970
But you can look at this
specific video and conclude

00:10:35.970 --> 00:10:38.280
that the actors probably
make the participants go

00:10:38.280 --> 00:10:39.590
through the whole waiver process

00:10:39.590 --> 00:10:42.880
again during the experience
for the show of it

00:10:42.880 --> 00:10:45.420
as part of the psychological
game that they play

00:10:45.420 --> 00:10:49.070
or they were just making the
actors sign fake waivers.

00:10:49.070 --> 00:10:50.480
But let's talk about another one

00:10:50.480 --> 00:10:53.360
of the requirements for this
crazy waiver to be valid.

00:10:53.360 --> 00:10:55.320
Which is that a waiver must be specific

00:10:55.320 --> 00:10:57.600
about known and unknown risks.

00:10:57.600 --> 00:10:59.610
All States have their own requirements

00:10:59.610 --> 00:11:00.940
when it comes to these sort of things.

00:11:00.940 --> 00:11:03.050
But whether a waiver will be enforceable,

00:11:03.050 --> 00:11:04.980
generally boils down to whether the injury

00:11:04.980 --> 00:11:07.540
arises from risks stated in the waiver.

00:11:07.540 --> 00:11:09.190
This means that the language of the waiver

00:11:09.190 --> 00:11:12.210
should be specific about
both known and unknown risks

00:11:12.210 --> 00:11:14.370
and the inherent risks of an activity.

00:11:14.370 --> 00:11:15.810
Starting with paragraph 17,

00:11:15.810 --> 00:11:18.140
the McKamey Manor waiver
starts listing all

00:11:18.140 --> 00:11:19.270
of the things that could happen

00:11:19.270 --> 00:11:21.640
to someone who participates
in McKamey Manor.

00:11:21.640 --> 00:11:23.410
There could be bumps, bruises cuts

00:11:23.410 --> 00:11:25.870
and other possible injuries,
including broken bones.

00:11:25.870 --> 00:11:28.700
Paragraph 18 asks that you
not fight with the actors.

00:11:28.700 --> 00:11:30.078
Well 19 warns that

00:11:30.078 --> 00:11:32.710
with respect to doing
the entire experience,

00:11:32.710 --> 00:11:36.060
you really don't want
to do this, in all caps.

00:11:36.060 --> 00:11:37.940
Paragraph 24 says that you understand

00:11:37.940 --> 00:11:39.580
you aren't being held against your will.

00:11:39.580 --> 00:11:41.410
And McKamey Manor is well aware

00:11:41.410 --> 00:11:43.570
that the neighbors may
misconstrue what's going on

00:11:43.570 --> 00:11:44.430
and call the police.

00:11:44.430 --> 00:11:47.100
That's why paragraph three
says the participant agrees

00:11:47.100 --> 00:11:49.070
that if the police are called
or appear on the scene,

00:11:49.070 --> 00:11:51.690
that participant acknowledges
that this is just a game.

00:11:51.690 --> 00:11:54.240
This idea that this is just a game

00:11:54.240 --> 00:11:56.590
is a common theme in this crazy contract.

00:11:56.590 --> 00:11:57.880
What else is in this contract?

00:11:57.880 --> 00:11:59.980
Forcibly shaved head and eyebrows.

00:11:59.980 --> 00:12:01.040
There's a paragraph for that.

00:12:01.040 --> 00:12:02.930
Stuffed inside a 55 gallon drum

00:12:02.930 --> 00:12:04.560
and rolled down a hill onto a pond,

00:12:04.560 --> 00:12:05.393
count on it.

00:12:05.393 --> 00:12:07.270
Buried in a pit with
hundreds of mice, rats

00:12:07.270 --> 00:12:08.103
and rodents.

00:12:08.103 --> 00:12:09.870
Your responsibility to fight through it.

00:12:09.870 --> 00:12:11.730
Knives in close contact with your body,

00:12:11.730 --> 00:12:12.563
even your neck.

00:12:12.563 --> 00:12:13.550
Definitely possible.

00:12:13.550 --> 00:12:14.383
Hypothermia.

00:12:14.383 --> 00:12:15.300
Sure. Why not?

00:12:15.300 --> 00:12:17.260
Hundreds of welts from a paintball gun.

00:12:17.260 --> 00:12:18.093
Obviously.

00:12:18.093 --> 00:12:19.550
Now in a stroke of genius,

00:12:19.550 --> 00:12:22.070
some of these scenarios are
probably just there to get

00:12:22.070 --> 00:12:24.560
inside your head, to
play on your worst fears

00:12:24.560 --> 00:12:26.060
like Russ McKamey promises.

00:12:26.060 --> 00:12:28.250
This would be the bits
about being buried alive.

00:12:28.250 --> 00:12:30.040
Submerged in 60 feet of water

00:12:30.040 --> 00:12:31.410
without a breathing apparatus.

00:12:31.410 --> 00:12:33.320
Walking a plank 25 feet off the ground

00:12:33.320 --> 00:12:34.170
without a safety net.

00:12:34.170 --> 00:12:35.570
Encountering poisonous animals

00:12:35.570 --> 00:12:37.240
or possible electrocution.

00:12:37.240 --> 00:12:38.570
This all sounds ridiculous

00:12:38.570 --> 00:12:40.680
but McKamey claims that
he's never been sued.

00:12:40.680 --> 00:12:42.730
But he also claims that he has been sued.

00:12:42.730 --> 00:12:44.480
And then all lawsuits have been thrown

00:12:44.480 --> 00:12:46.480
out once he reveals the
videos of what happened.

00:12:46.480 --> 00:12:48.760
So we're dealing with some
inconsistent information.

00:12:48.760 --> 00:12:51.630
But is this waiver good in a court of law?

00:12:51.630 --> 00:12:52.810
Well that requires digging

00:12:52.810 --> 00:12:55.521
into something called a
pre-injury release waiver.

00:12:55.521 --> 00:12:58.960
McKamey Manor's waiver is
such a pre-injury release.

00:12:58.960 --> 00:13:01.180
This is a written document
that a participant signs

00:13:01.180 --> 00:13:03.840
before engaging in a
recreational activity.

00:13:03.840 --> 00:13:05.150
The document is a contract which

00:13:05.150 --> 00:13:07.080
releases the service provider from claims

00:13:07.080 --> 00:13:08.610
and individual may bring as a result

00:13:08.610 --> 00:13:09.950
of the providers negligence.

00:13:09.950 --> 00:13:11.640
You typically sign a pre-injury release

00:13:11.640 --> 00:13:14.670
for skydiving, bungee
jumping and trampoline parks.

00:13:14.670 --> 00:13:16.460
This release feature liability

00:13:16.460 --> 00:13:17.860
is a contractual agreement

00:13:17.860 --> 00:13:19.740
where one party surrenders legal rights

00:13:19.740 --> 00:13:20.600
or obligations.

00:13:20.600 --> 00:13:22.000
Though generally a valid release

00:13:22.000 --> 00:13:24.230
continues to be a complete bar to recovery

00:13:24.230 --> 00:13:26.850
and negligence actions in
almost every jurisdiction.

00:13:26.850 --> 00:13:28.860
But what courts consider
to be a valid release

00:13:28.860 --> 00:13:30.330
varies from state to state.

00:13:30.330 --> 00:13:31.890
Here's an example of the most recent

00:13:31.890 --> 00:13:32.950
McKamey Manor waiver.

00:13:32.950 --> 00:13:33.900
Is from 2017,

00:13:33.900 --> 00:13:35.320
the jurisdiction is California.

00:13:35.320 --> 00:13:36.660
So for purposes of this video

00:13:36.660 --> 00:13:38.380
I'll discuss California, Tennessee

00:13:38.380 --> 00:13:40.170
and Alabama law together.

00:13:40.170 --> 00:13:42.710
Since pre-injury releases
are valid and enforceable

00:13:42.710 --> 00:13:46.200
in all three States under
generally similar circles.

00:13:46.200 --> 00:13:48.040
Though generally a waiver is unenforceable

00:13:48.040 --> 00:13:50.270
if there's fraud or willful injury.

00:13:50.270 --> 00:13:51.270
Now you might be thinking,

00:13:51.270 --> 00:13:52.710
but isn't that exactly

00:13:52.710 --> 00:13:54.240
what McKamey Manor is doing?

00:13:54.240 --> 00:13:56.030
Intentionally causing injury.

00:13:56.030 --> 00:13:56.980
Well not so fast

00:13:56.980 --> 00:13:58.640
because the contractual language goes

00:13:58.640 --> 00:14:00.850
into explicit detail about the harm

00:14:00.850 --> 00:14:01.683
that may be for you.

00:14:01.683 --> 00:14:03.510
But always with the caveat
that this is the type

00:14:03.510 --> 00:14:05.120
of harm that would be an accident

00:14:05.120 --> 00:14:06.690
and is no way intentionally

00:14:06.690 --> 00:14:08.450
or most importantly willful.

00:14:08.450 --> 00:14:10.510
The waiver requires participants to agree

00:14:10.510 --> 00:14:12.080
that their lives are not in danger

00:14:12.080 --> 00:14:12.970
and it's just a game.

00:14:12.970 --> 00:14:14.440
Participants are supposed to realize

00:14:14.440 --> 00:14:15.930
that they aren't really being tortured

00:14:15.930 --> 00:14:17.860
even if it feels like they are.

00:14:17.860 --> 00:14:19.140
It says that you'll be roughed up

00:14:19.140 --> 00:14:21.230
but no one is there to actually hurt you.

00:14:21.230 --> 00:14:22.910
This is McKameys's way of saying that

00:14:22.910 --> 00:14:25.330
an injury is accidental and not willful.

00:14:25.330 --> 00:14:27.840
But then there's the issue of negligence.

00:14:27.840 --> 00:14:30.010
And whether you can
wave a negligence claim.

00:14:30.010 --> 00:14:31.500
The legal system defines negligence

00:14:31.500 --> 00:14:33.810
as the failure to exercise
the care towards others

00:14:33.810 --> 00:14:35.740
which are reasonable or
prudent person would do

00:14:35.740 --> 00:14:37.540
in the same or similar circumstances.

00:14:37.540 --> 00:14:39.810
And to prove negligence
lawyers have to show

00:14:39.810 --> 00:14:40.810
that a person being sued

00:14:40.810 --> 00:14:42.620
had a duty of care to another person.

00:14:42.620 --> 00:14:44.260
That they breached that duty

00:14:44.260 --> 00:14:46.620
and that the breach caused
the plaintiff's injuries

00:14:46.620 --> 00:14:47.960
By waiving negligence claims,

00:14:47.960 --> 00:14:49.880
people who participate in McKamey Manor

00:14:49.880 --> 00:14:51.370
are giving up their right to sue

00:14:51.370 --> 00:14:53.730
if McCainy acts unreasonably.

00:14:53.730 --> 00:14:55.810
But can someone simply waive claims

00:14:55.810 --> 00:14:57.100
of ordinary negligence?

00:14:57.100 --> 00:14:59.020
Well, yes, almost always you can.

00:14:59.020 --> 00:15:01.370
Unless the process of
getting the waiver signed

00:15:01.370 --> 00:15:02.330
was somehow fraudulent

00:15:02.330 --> 00:15:04.637
or the person who signed
the release was a minor.

00:15:04.637 --> 00:15:07.910
The parties can waive their
right to sue for negligence.

00:15:07.910 --> 00:15:09.320
As long as the waiver accurately

00:15:09.320 --> 00:15:10.850
expresses the intent of the parties

00:15:10.850 --> 00:15:13.300
and the injuries reasonably
included in the type

00:15:13.300 --> 00:15:15.540
of thing that the waiver
was intended to cover,

00:15:15.540 --> 00:15:17.950
that negligence waiver
will probably be upheld.

00:15:17.950 --> 00:15:19.070
People who've tried to sue

00:15:19.070 --> 00:15:20.610
haunted attractions for negligence

00:15:20.610 --> 00:15:21.570
have been kicked out of court

00:15:21.570 --> 00:15:23.960
because they waived
their negligence claims.

00:15:23.960 --> 00:15:26.150
For example, in 2011, Scott Griffin

00:15:26.150 --> 00:15:28.510
and friends went to the
Haunted Trail in San Diego.

00:15:28.510 --> 00:15:30.710
The haunted trail was a mile long path

00:15:30.710 --> 00:15:32.480
where visitors were chased by actors

00:15:32.480 --> 00:15:34.930
dressed in scary costumes,
carrying fake weapons.

00:15:34.930 --> 00:15:36.390
And the tickets that they purchased

00:15:36.390 --> 00:15:38.480
had a warning that said
the trail included quote

00:15:38.480 --> 00:15:40.310
high impact scares.

00:15:40.310 --> 00:15:41.170
Griffin and his friends

00:15:41.170 --> 00:15:42.620
made it through the
trail with no problems.

00:15:42.620 --> 00:15:44.690
But as they walked out to the exit,

00:15:44.690 --> 00:15:47.080
an actor carrying a revving chainsaw

00:15:47.080 --> 00:15:48.080
rushed towards them.

00:15:48.080 --> 00:15:50.040
Griffin made what some
would call a rookie mistake,

00:15:50.040 --> 00:15:51.530
thinking that the attraction was over

00:15:51.530 --> 00:15:53.150
before he was back in his car.

00:15:53.150 --> 00:15:55.417
He panicked and fell injuring both wrists,

00:15:55.417 --> 00:15:57.520
which were in cast for four months.

00:15:57.520 --> 00:15:59.350
The court dismissed the negligence lawsuit

00:15:59.350 --> 00:16:00.740
because of the ticket disclaimer.

00:16:00.740 --> 00:16:03.230
Quote, being chased within
the physical confines

00:16:03.230 --> 00:16:05.790
of the Haunted Trail by a
chainsaw carrying maniac

00:16:05.790 --> 00:16:06.810
is a fundamental part

00:16:06.810 --> 00:16:09.030
and inherent risk of this amusement.

00:16:09.030 --> 00:16:11.540
Griffin voluntarily paid
money to experience it.

00:16:11.540 --> 00:16:13.180
As the Griffin case demonstrates,

00:16:13.180 --> 00:16:15.140
a plaintiff goes through
a Halloween attraction

00:16:15.140 --> 00:16:17.410
for the purpose of being scared.

00:16:17.410 --> 00:16:18.243
- You scared me dog.

00:16:18.243 --> 00:16:19.830
- That's the point, is it?

00:16:19.830 --> 00:16:21.380
- It is, it is and you're doing well

00:16:21.380 --> 00:16:23.050
- As long as the person
is sufficiently warned

00:16:23.050 --> 00:16:24.510
about the potential hazards prior

00:16:24.510 --> 00:16:26.040
to entering a certain space,

00:16:26.040 --> 00:16:28.390
courts probably won't
hold the business liable

00:16:28.390 --> 00:16:29.889
for injuries caused by mistakes

00:16:29.889 --> 00:16:31.030
the business makes.

00:16:31.030 --> 00:16:32.440
Or the injuries that might result

00:16:32.440 --> 00:16:34.070
from fear-based reactions.

00:16:34.070 --> 00:16:35.970
And for an example of basic negligence,

00:16:35.970 --> 00:16:39.320
consider the case of Mays
versus Gretna Athletic Boosters.

00:16:39.320 --> 00:16:42.030
There, a Louisiana business
got sued for using a lot

00:16:42.030 --> 00:16:45.100
of black visqueen material
inside of the haunted house.

00:16:45.100 --> 00:16:46.400
A ten-year-old girl got frightened

00:16:46.400 --> 00:16:48.300
and ran straight into a cinder block wall,

00:16:48.300 --> 00:16:49.430
breaking her nose.

00:16:49.430 --> 00:16:50.360
Now, even if the business

00:16:50.360 --> 00:16:52.487
arguably made the haunted house too dark,

00:16:52.487 --> 00:16:54.110
so dark that she couldn't tell

00:16:54.110 --> 00:16:55.600
it was a cinder block wall.

00:16:55.600 --> 00:16:57.560
The negligence waiver was upheld.

00:16:57.560 --> 00:16:58.430
That's because the business

00:16:58.430 --> 00:17:00.180
didn't intend for this to happen.

00:17:00.180 --> 00:17:01.660
It may have made a mistake

00:17:01.660 --> 00:17:03.510
but generally the building's construction

00:17:03.510 --> 00:17:04.343
were sound.

00:17:04.343 --> 00:17:05.176
Haunted house operators

00:17:05.176 --> 00:17:06.500
are duty bound to protect patrons

00:17:06.500 --> 00:17:08.600
only from unreasonably
dangerous conditions

00:17:08.600 --> 00:17:11.200
and probably not from
every conceivable danger.

00:17:11.200 --> 00:17:13.010
Another example of a fear-based reaction

00:17:13.010 --> 00:17:14.100
happened in Louisiana.

00:17:14.100 --> 00:17:15.350
Where the plaintiff broke her leg

00:17:15.350 --> 00:17:17.160
in a corn maze while running from an actor

00:17:17.160 --> 00:17:19.390
dressed as Jason from Friday the 13th.

00:17:19.390 --> 00:17:21.076
The court ruled that she
was explicitly warned

00:17:21.076 --> 00:17:24.230
that she might be chased
by scaring looking actors.

00:17:24.230 --> 00:17:26.350
Quote we find that no duty was owed

00:17:26.350 --> 00:17:28.280
by the Billingses to Mrs.Durmon

00:17:28.280 --> 00:17:30.390
in this case to warn or protect her

00:17:30.390 --> 00:17:32.230
from her reaction to being frightened

00:17:32.230 --> 00:17:33.063
by Jason,

00:17:33.063 --> 00:17:35.250
an experience she expected to have

00:17:35.250 --> 00:17:37.670
and for which he paid an
additional admission fee.

00:17:37.670 --> 00:17:39.870
But there is an exception to this rule

00:17:39.870 --> 00:17:41.260
and that's for gross negligence

00:17:41.260 --> 00:17:42.320
and recklessness.

00:17:42.320 --> 00:17:43.710
Such a contractual waiver

00:17:43.710 --> 00:17:45.890
won't protect McKamey Manor if it's guilty

00:17:45.890 --> 00:17:47.730
of what's called gross negligence.

00:17:47.730 --> 00:17:48.870
What is gross negligence?

00:17:48.870 --> 00:17:51.360
And how is it different
from regular negligence?

00:17:51.360 --> 00:17:52.280
Well, gross negligence

00:17:52.280 --> 00:17:54.750
generally involves a higher
degree of bad conduct

00:17:54.750 --> 00:17:56.720
and callous indifference to consequences.

00:17:56.720 --> 00:17:58.790
In Tennessee, where McKamey now lives,

00:17:58.790 --> 00:18:00.210
the law doesn't let people contract

00:18:00.210 --> 00:18:01.990
away liability for gross negligence.

00:18:01.990 --> 00:18:04.010
However, it's often hard to prove

00:18:04.010 --> 00:18:05.620
that someone committed gross negligence.

00:18:05.620 --> 00:18:06.570
You'd have to prove one,

00:18:06.570 --> 00:18:08.360
that the person committed
ordinary negligence.

00:18:08.360 --> 00:18:11.160
And two, the person acted
with utter unconcern

00:18:11.160 --> 00:18:12.220
for the safety of others

00:18:12.220 --> 00:18:15.000
or with such a reckless disregard
for the rights of others.

00:18:15.000 --> 00:18:18.600
The conscious indifference to
the consequences is implied.

00:18:18.600 --> 00:18:20.910
A good example of gross
negligence occurred in Kentucky

00:18:20.910 --> 00:18:22.930
where Glenda Dickson broke her back

00:18:22.930 --> 00:18:25.510
in four places after falling
out of a second story window

00:18:25.510 --> 00:18:26.520
in a haunted house.

00:18:26.520 --> 00:18:27.590
Dickson signed a waiver

00:18:27.590 --> 00:18:29.500
and knew that the house would be scary.

00:18:29.500 --> 00:18:30.720
However, when she was near

00:18:30.720 --> 00:18:32.231
a second story open window,

00:18:32.231 --> 00:18:34.670
one of the actors ran behind Dickson

00:18:34.670 --> 00:18:35.503
and her group screaming

00:18:35.503 --> 00:18:37.410
causing everyone to jump in terror

00:18:37.410 --> 00:18:38.710
Dickson jumped backwards

00:18:38.710 --> 00:18:40.490
and fell out of the open window.

00:18:40.490 --> 00:18:41.830
The window was covered by a sheet

00:18:41.830 --> 00:18:44.340
and Dickson had no idea
it was actually open.

00:18:44.340 --> 00:18:46.500
In this case there was
ordinary negligence.

00:18:46.500 --> 00:18:48.670
An actor scaring a person
near an open window

00:18:48.670 --> 00:18:51.640
causes reckless disregard
for what may happen to others

00:18:51.640 --> 00:18:53.480
as a result of the negligence.

00:18:53.480 --> 00:18:55.053
And a reasonable haunted house

00:18:55.053 --> 00:18:56.960
would have shut the window

00:18:56.960 --> 00:18:58.060
that was covered with a sheet

00:18:58.060 --> 00:18:59.570
before scaring someone who stood

00:18:59.570 --> 00:19:01.530
near it to prevent defenestration.

00:19:01.530 --> 00:19:03.450
So even though some people are horrified

00:19:03.450 --> 00:19:05.380
by the footage of McKamey Manor,

00:19:05.380 --> 00:19:07.310
it'd be hard to argue that McKamey

00:19:07.310 --> 00:19:09.520
was being misleading
or failing to warn them

00:19:09.520 --> 00:19:11.210
about the hazards that they might face.

00:19:11.210 --> 00:19:14.530
Now there's one final
question about McKamey Manor.

00:19:14.530 --> 00:19:16.110
And it's probably the question you've had

00:19:16.110 --> 00:19:17.210
throughout this entire video.

00:19:17.210 --> 00:19:19.170
Which is, can a person actually

00:19:19.170 --> 00:19:21.570
consent to legitimately harmful acts

00:19:21.570 --> 00:19:23.030
being perpetrated against them?

00:19:23.030 --> 00:19:24.430
And a person on (indistinct)

00:19:24.430 --> 00:19:27.210
whether it was legal for
McKamey Manor to kill a person.

00:19:27.210 --> 00:19:28.930
And the answer to that is no.

00:19:28.930 --> 00:19:30.960
If someone died during the experience,

00:19:30.960 --> 00:19:32.020
then it would be possible

00:19:32.020 --> 00:19:33.380
that their family would have a valid

00:19:33.380 --> 00:19:36.110
legal claim against McKamey
Manor for wrongful death.

00:19:36.110 --> 00:19:38.540
Even if they sign the
waiver without coercion.

00:19:38.540 --> 00:19:42.197
Unless the death resulted
from very ordinary negligence.

00:19:42.197 --> 00:19:44.139
Let's say someone died from hypothermia.

00:19:44.139 --> 00:19:46.520
The waiver warns that
hypothermia is a risk

00:19:46.520 --> 00:19:48.810
but it also says if
hypothermia is detected

00:19:48.810 --> 00:19:50.800
the person will be pulled out immediately.

00:19:50.800 --> 00:19:53.620
If McKamey Manor failed to
detect it and someone died

00:19:53.620 --> 00:19:56.237
their estate might be able
to sue for gross negligence.

00:19:56.237 --> 00:19:58.700
And not all deaths would lead to a claim.

00:19:58.700 --> 00:20:00.210
If someone signed the waiver

00:20:00.210 --> 00:20:01.630
and produced documents
saying that they were

00:20:01.630 --> 00:20:03.720
in good health and they
actually had a heart problem

00:20:03.720 --> 00:20:05.040
and that person had a heart attack,

00:20:05.040 --> 00:20:07.430
McKamey Manor wouldn't
necessarily be liable.

00:20:07.430 --> 00:20:09.300
It depends heavily on the facts.

00:20:09.300 --> 00:20:11.790
- They're just asking to have someone die

00:20:11.790 --> 00:20:12.770
or have a heart attack.

00:20:12.770 --> 00:20:15.340
What happens if someone
dies during their tour?

00:20:15.340 --> 00:20:16.740
It waver won't mean anything.

00:20:16.740 --> 00:20:19.460
We have had one heart attack for real.

00:20:19.460 --> 00:20:21.330
Seven years ago, we had a heart attack.

00:20:21.330 --> 00:20:22.390
That was good stuff.

00:20:22.390 --> 00:20:23.740
- And the most common question

00:20:23.740 --> 00:20:25.820
people have about McKamey Manor

00:20:25.820 --> 00:20:28.050
is whether it's legal
for someone to consent

00:20:28.050 --> 00:20:31.160
to being hurt maimed or even
killed at a fright attraction.

00:20:31.160 --> 00:20:31.993
And unsurprisingly,

00:20:31.993 --> 00:20:34.870
the answer is both no and yes.

00:20:34.870 --> 00:20:36.000
In most jurisdictions,

00:20:36.000 --> 00:20:38.650
consent is a defense to
crimes that don't result

00:20:38.650 --> 00:20:41.110
in serious injury or happen
during sporting events.

00:20:41.110 --> 00:20:43.203
So if we're talking about serious injury,

00:20:43.203 --> 00:20:46.160
no McKamey Manor can't
intentionally kill someone

00:20:46.160 --> 00:20:48.300
or cause them egregious bodily harm

00:20:48.300 --> 00:20:49.790
even if they sign the waiver.

00:20:49.790 --> 00:20:51.340
It isn't legal for McKamey Manor

00:20:51.340 --> 00:20:53.430
to let participants
drown or freeze to death

00:20:53.430 --> 00:20:54.860
no matter what the waiver says.

00:20:54.860 --> 00:20:56.370
That's against public policy.

00:20:56.370 --> 00:20:57.870
So now, you know, the general rule,

00:20:57.870 --> 00:21:01.580
consent cannot be given a
serious harmful criminal act.

00:21:01.580 --> 00:21:03.330
But as with most things in the law,

00:21:03.330 --> 00:21:04.876
there are some exceptions.

00:21:04.876 --> 00:21:06.550
A person generally is allowed

00:21:06.550 --> 00:21:07.890
to consent to serious harm

00:21:07.890 --> 00:21:09.530
if the harm is for the purposes of

00:21:09.530 --> 00:21:11.394
medical treatment like amputating a leg.

00:21:11.394 --> 00:21:13.597
They could also consent
to being punched or kicked

00:21:13.597 --> 00:21:15.710
or even attacked as long as they are

00:21:15.710 --> 00:21:16.960
in a legally sanctioned event

00:21:16.960 --> 00:21:19.040
such as boxing or mixed martial art.

00:21:19.040 --> 00:21:19.873
And this is legal

00:21:19.873 --> 00:21:20.990
even though these events could result

00:21:20.990 --> 00:21:22.300
in serious harm or even death.

00:21:22.300 --> 00:21:23.760
Boxers have been killed in the ring.

00:21:23.760 --> 00:21:25.250
But the participants and the promoters

00:21:25.250 --> 00:21:27.210
generally don't have
a legal responsibility

00:21:27.210 --> 00:21:29.320
for murder or manslaughter

00:21:29.320 --> 00:21:31.460
that might happen in a boxing ring.

00:21:31.460 --> 00:21:33.450
Boxing is a state regulated activity

00:21:33.450 --> 00:21:35.170
that's lawful unless the death happens

00:21:35.170 --> 00:21:36.890
because of something
unrelated to the fight.

00:21:36.890 --> 00:21:38.806
Like the roof collapsing in the ring.

00:21:38.806 --> 00:21:40.529
So for this exception to apply,

00:21:40.529 --> 00:21:43.480
the issue is whether the
event is legally permissible.

00:21:43.480 --> 00:21:46.120
A person may not consent
to a duel for instance,

00:21:46.120 --> 00:21:47.800
because dueling has been outlawed.

00:21:47.800 --> 00:21:49.190
A person cannot legally consent to

00:21:49.190 --> 00:21:50.520
a street fighter or a bar room brawl

00:21:50.520 --> 00:21:52.320
even if involves the same kind of conduct

00:21:52.320 --> 00:21:53.160
as an MMA match.

00:21:53.160 --> 00:21:54.620
Because isn't legal to get

00:21:54.620 --> 00:21:56.160
into a gang fight or a bar fight.

00:21:56.160 --> 00:21:57.680
But there might be some exceptions

00:21:57.680 --> 00:21:59.530
to certain kinds of assault and battery.

00:21:59.530 --> 00:22:00.363
Assault is the crime

00:22:00.363 --> 00:22:01.610
of putting another person in fear or

00:22:01.610 --> 00:22:03.410
apprehension of an unlawful contact.

00:22:03.410 --> 00:22:05.040
Battery is the actual application

00:22:05.040 --> 00:22:05.930
of that force;

00:22:05.930 --> 00:22:07.490
a harmful or offensive contact

00:22:07.490 --> 00:22:08.640
with another person's body.

00:22:08.640 --> 00:22:10.618
Assault and battery are crimes and torts.

00:22:10.618 --> 00:22:12.130
That means a person can be charged

00:22:12.130 --> 00:22:14.665
with a crime or sued for
committing assault or battery,

00:22:14.665 --> 00:22:16.061
get monetary damages.

00:22:16.061 --> 00:22:17.881
Participants in an athletic contest

00:22:17.881 --> 00:22:20.600
are deemed to have consented
to certain assaults

00:22:20.600 --> 00:22:22.480
and batteries when both the injury

00:22:22.480 --> 00:22:23.940
and the conduct that caused it

00:22:23.940 --> 00:22:26.170
are reasonably foreseeable hazards

00:22:26.170 --> 00:22:28.800
of joint participation
in an athletic contest.

00:22:28.800 --> 00:22:31.050
So when is the conduct
reasonably foreseeable?

00:22:31.050 --> 00:22:32.850
This isn't always easy to pin down.

00:22:32.850 --> 00:22:34.840
NHL hockey player Marty McSorley,

00:22:34.840 --> 00:22:36.680
was found guilty of assault with a weapon

00:22:36.680 --> 00:22:38.360
after he hit Donald Brashear

00:22:38.360 --> 00:22:39.880
in the head with a hockey stick,

00:22:39.880 --> 00:22:41.610
when Brashear was skating away from him.

00:22:41.610 --> 00:22:43.370
The court rejected McSorley's defense

00:22:43.370 --> 00:22:45.870
that NHL players give explicit consent

00:22:45.870 --> 00:22:47.840
to the risk of on ice contact.

00:22:47.840 --> 00:22:50.060
And that McSorleys's
hit was not an assault.

00:22:50.060 --> 00:22:51.865
Hockey fights are a foreseeable risk.

00:22:51.865 --> 00:22:53.935
But intentionally striking someone

00:22:53.935 --> 00:22:55.770
in the head head with a hockey stick

00:22:55.770 --> 00:22:57.690
is considered assault with a deadly weapon

00:22:57.690 --> 00:22:59.250
and that's not a foreseeable risk.

00:22:59.250 --> 00:23:00.610
Still most jurisdictions have been

00:23:00.610 --> 00:23:02.010
very hesitant to prosecute players

00:23:02.010 --> 00:23:04.130
even when their behavior
seems to cross a line.

00:23:04.130 --> 00:23:05.730
In another hockey case, a New York court

00:23:05.730 --> 00:23:07.690
let a player strike
another player in the neck,

00:23:07.690 --> 00:23:09.290
slamming him into the goalpost

00:23:09.290 --> 00:23:10.750
even after the whistle was blown.

00:23:10.750 --> 00:23:12.120
And the court thought that the victim

00:23:12.120 --> 00:23:13.880
assumed the risk by participating

00:23:13.880 --> 00:23:15.070
in the hockey event

00:23:15.070 --> 00:23:16.200
even though it was a league that

00:23:16.200 --> 00:23:17.980
didn't allow players to
cross-check each other.

00:23:17.980 --> 00:23:19.610
The court thought that
policy considerations

00:23:19.610 --> 00:23:21.920
of having quote free
and fierce competition

00:23:21.920 --> 00:23:23.350
in athletic events, end quote,

00:23:23.350 --> 00:23:24.510
would be severely undermined

00:23:24.510 --> 00:23:26.590
if the usual criminal
standards were applied

00:23:26.590 --> 00:23:28.842
to athletic competition,
especially ice hockey.

00:23:28.842 --> 00:23:30.060
So it's possible

00:23:30.060 --> 00:23:31.610
that a court could accept the analogy

00:23:31.610 --> 00:23:34.110
and say that if we let
consenting adults engage

00:23:34.110 --> 00:23:36.300
in what would otherwise
be criminal behavior

00:23:36.300 --> 00:23:37.133
in a sporting event,

00:23:37.133 --> 00:23:38.770
then we should absolutely let them consent

00:23:38.770 --> 00:23:41.162
to such behavior at a
haunted house as well.

00:23:41.162 --> 00:23:43.760
But the court could
also reject that analogy

00:23:43.760 --> 00:23:46.210
and say that there are pro-social reasons

00:23:46.210 --> 00:23:48.870
why we want to encourage
people participating

00:23:48.870 --> 00:23:50.802
in sporting events that simply don't exist

00:23:50.802 --> 00:23:53.802
for something as frivolous
as a haunted house.

00:23:53.802 --> 00:23:55.930
And that all the safeguards that exist

00:23:55.930 --> 00:23:57.762
in a sporting event like
referees and umpires

00:23:57.762 --> 00:24:00.450
and the spectators that
actually prevent things

00:24:00.450 --> 00:24:01.460
from going too far,

00:24:01.460 --> 00:24:03.840
don't exist in the case
of a haunted house.

00:24:03.840 --> 00:24:05.430
And it's just too likely

00:24:05.430 --> 00:24:07.022
that things are going to go off the rails

00:24:07.022 --> 00:24:09.030
for entertainment purposes.

00:24:09.030 --> 00:24:10.470
So what's my verdict.

00:24:10.470 --> 00:24:12.690
Well, arguably the waiver is legal.

00:24:12.690 --> 00:24:14.010
At least for a lot of the things

00:24:14.010 --> 00:24:15.500
that it's trying to disclaim

00:24:15.500 --> 00:24:17.240
and probably so is the attraction

00:24:17.240 --> 00:24:19.550
unless there's a lot
of unknown information

00:24:19.550 --> 00:24:20.410
that we don't have.

00:24:20.410 --> 00:24:21.600
A few caveats though,

00:24:21.600 --> 00:24:23.680
it's notable that McKamey has stopped

00:24:23.680 --> 00:24:25.870
the two most legally
questionable practices

00:24:25.870 --> 00:24:26.980
which is using minors

00:24:26.980 --> 00:24:28.860
and not having a safe word or phrase.

00:24:28.860 --> 00:24:31.500
In the past McKamey used
local teenagers as actors

00:24:31.500 --> 00:24:33.200
and now he hires only adults.

00:24:33.200 --> 00:24:35.320
If a participant absolutely
can't stand it anymore,

00:24:35.320 --> 00:24:37.550
they can use a safe phrase
to stop the experience.

00:24:37.550 --> 00:24:39.250
At least that's what we think based

00:24:39.250 --> 00:24:40.600
on the available evidence.

00:24:40.600 --> 00:24:42.120
The Nashville scene has reported

00:24:42.120 --> 00:24:44.170
that McKamey now has an
arrangement with the police.

00:24:44.170 --> 00:24:46.140
He calls when he's about to give a tour.

00:24:46.140 --> 00:24:47.760
Some people think that
none of this is real

00:24:47.760 --> 00:24:49.430
and that most of the videos
on the YouTube channel

00:24:49.430 --> 00:24:51.130
feature actors not participants.

00:24:51.130 --> 00:24:52.650
And having watched a few hours of it,

00:24:52.650 --> 00:24:54.200
that's totally plausible,

00:24:54.200 --> 00:24:55.490
though definitely not certain.

00:24:55.490 --> 00:24:56.820
And frankly this may all

00:24:56.820 --> 00:24:59.330
just be a giant ploy for free advertising

00:24:59.330 --> 00:25:01.090
because I've done an entire freaking video

00:25:01.090 --> 00:25:02.840
on this stupid legal contract.

00:25:02.840 --> 00:25:04.720
And McKamey is a master of illusion

00:25:04.720 --> 00:25:06.570
and video editing so
nothing is certain here.

00:25:06.570 --> 00:25:07.990
There are only two ways to get answers.

00:25:07.990 --> 00:25:09.170
One is either sue him

00:25:09.170 --> 00:25:10.728
and see what he's got
to say in a deposition

00:25:10.728 --> 00:25:12.810
or go through the experience myself.

00:25:12.810 --> 00:25:13.920
And I think I'll pass on

00:25:13.920 --> 00:25:15.287
both of those options for now.

00:25:15.287 --> 00:25:17.370
(laughs)

